## Thread.Abort Migration to .NET 8

### Pattern Analysis

Your code contains **4 patterns** that must change:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException catch | `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| Force-kill via Abort | `_workerThread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` + `Join` with timeout |

**Why this breaks on .NET 8:** `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it does nothing. Your `Stop()` method will never forcibly terminate the worker.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — migrated from ThreadAbortException catch
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we control the loop via the token.
                // Loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop in time. Unlike Thread.Abort(), there is
            // no forced kill — the thread must cooperate. Log a warning.
            Console.Error.WriteLine("Worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

1. **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** — This is the biggest risk. If it blocks on I/O or a `WaitHandle`, cancellation never takes effect. You **must** pass the token into it:
   ```csharp
   // If it uses BlockingCollection<T>:
   var item = _queue.Take(token);  // throws OperationCanceledException

   // If it uses ManualResetEvent:
   WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _dataReady, token.WaitHandle });

   // If it uses Thread.Sleep:
   token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval); // replaces Thread.Sleep
   ```

2. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException` broadly** — The `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch *your* cancellation, not an unrelated one from `ProcessItem`.

3. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — It's `IDisposable`. Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The `IDisposable` pattern on the class handles this.

4. **No forced kill exists** — Unlike `Thread.Abort`, there is no fallback. If `ProcessItem` runs for 10 minutes without checking the token, cancellation waits 10 minutes. Add checkpoints:
   ```csharp
   void ProcessItem(Item item, CancellationToken token)
   {
       StepOne(item);
       token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
       StepTwo(item);
       token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
       StepThree(item);
   }
   ```

5. **`volatile bool _running` is removed** — `CancellationToken` replaces it entirely. Don't mix both mechanisms.
